= Jiahui Cheng

~~~
{}{img_left}{./avatar/avatar.jpg}{Jiahui Cheng}{260px}{260px}
~~~

I am a *Research Scientist* at [https://about.meta.com Meta Platforms, Inc.], working on machine learning at production scale.\n
\n
Before Meta I completed my Ph.D. in Mathematics at the [https://www.math.gatech.edu/ Georgia Institute of Technology], where I was very fortunate to be advised by [https://people.math.gatech.edu/~wliao60/ Wenjing Liao]. I received my bachelor's degree from the [https://en.scgy.ustc.edu.cn/ School of the Gifted Young] at the University of Science and Technology of China (USTC), where I worked with Weihua Tong on continuum simulation.\n
\n
My research sits between /statistical learning theory/ and /scientific machine learning/: I like questions where you can write down what a method is actually estimating, prove something about it, and then watch that proof tell you which knob to turn in practice.\n
\n
I write up what I read and what I get stuck on over at [thoughts.html *Thoughts*].

== What I Work On

=== Why deep networks work as well as they do
Most approximation theory for neural networks assumes the target function has one uniform smoothness everywhere, and that data arrive uniformly over the domain. Real signals are not like that: they are smooth in some places, kinked or discontinuous in others, and the data pile up in a few regions and thin out elsewhere. In [https://arxiv.org/pdf/2406.05320 recent work] we develop approximation and estimation theory for a function class defined by /nonlinear tree-based approximation/, which is rich enough to contain both uniformly regular and discontinuous functions, and show that deep ReLU networks adapt to varying regularity and to nonuniform data distributions at different locations and scales. The practical reading: the network does not need to be told where the hard parts are.

=== Learning dynamics from data
Given noisy samples of a trajectory, can you recover the equation that produced them? Two things make this hard. Candidate feature libraries need high-order derivatives, and numerical differentiation amplifies noise brutally; and a single trajectory may simply not excite enough of the dynamics to identify it. Our work on [https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.02848 {{<font color=red><b>}}PDE identification{{</b></font>}}] uses weak (integral) formulations to move derivatives off the data and onto smooth test functions, and uses /ensemble dynamics/ -- several trajectories at once -- to condition the resulting regression problem. A related question, on the linear side, is which eigenvalues of a system matrix are recoverable from partial observations of one trajectory, and under exactly what conditions.

=== Generative modeling, and making it cheap
Diffusion models produce beautiful samples and then charge you hundreds of network evaluations for each one. [https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.01469 {{<font color=red><b>}}Distillation{{</b></font>}}] compresses that sampler into a handful of steps, or one. I am interested in what survives that compression and what quietly does not: mode coverage, controllability mid-trajectory, and whether our evaluation metrics are even sensitive to the difference. See [thoughts-distillation.html my notes on this].

=== High-dimensional phenomena
Overparametrized models break the intuitions built in the classical regime. Interpolating models generalize; imbalanced training data can beat balanced data; and the peak in the risk curve that everyone points at turns out to be, largely, a story about missing regularization. I have worked on the [https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.00700 label-shift phase transition] for high-dimensional binary classification, and I keep coming back to the [https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.08560 double descent] literature. More in [thoughts-double-descent.html these notes].

